ges really two  pages should be the absolute maximum i  would recommend you to only work with  one page but six pages is just not okay  so let's just see what this person did  here and here first of all let's look at  the alignment i mean here again the  bullets start to the right then further  down it starts here to the left and then  if we go down we see things like this  for instance whether suddenly something  starts further to the right i mean this  is surely not okay this is not what i  would recommend you to do then some  other small sloppy things in the  formatting let's notice how here this  longer dash is used i believe it's  called an m dash whereas here a shorter  dash and n dash as i believe it's called  is used these are just sloppy small  things in the formatting then of course  you should rather avoid next let's talk  about the professional experience and  here first let's talk also about the  order of the sections so i'd say the  general recommended way is that as long  as you are a student you start your cv  after the personal details with the  education section because surely this is  the focus for you as a student but as  soon as you are a professional a working  professional who moves out of university  i'd say it's customary then to move up  the professional experience section and  then only afterwards show the education  section because surely this will usually  be what will be more relevant to the  person reading the cv what you should  definitely avoid is writing about these  experiences like you would write a  paragraph in a book so just full  sentences full paragraph of text it's  just really difficult just to quickly  read over this skim through it and just  in a straightforward way get the most  important pieces of information out of  it it really forces the people to read  through all of this and this even  combined with the fact that the cv in  itself is already so long it's just so  annoying just such a hassle for the  person looking 